@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: which bytes arriving on stdin are a terminal ANSWERING, which of those an agent may see, and how the child's OWN output decides the mouse
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/inputfilter.go, internal/ptyproxy/inputfilter_test.go
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy.go, internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy_test.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/main.go
'   the pty the filter sits on ..... _spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy.puml
'   the tap that recorded all this . _spec/internal/agentio/agent-terminal-io.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE RECOGNISED SET IS CLOSED, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS A KEYSTROKE ───────
' `classifyTerminalReport` names the sequences it knows and calls everything
' else `reportNone`, which is forwarded. The asymmetry is deliberate and runs
' the safe way: an unrecognised REPORT reaching the agent is the bug this file
' documents, and an unrecognised KEYSTROKE eaten is a worse one. Every gap found
' so far — the kitty pair, the mouse encodings — was closed by ADDING a case,
' never by widening a default.
'
' The whole set, so a future gap is visible as an absence rather than guessed at:
'   reportFocus ... `CSI I` / `CSI O`, a body of exactly one byte (DEC 1004).
'   reportReply ... `CSI ? … c` and `CSI > … c` — Primary/Secondary Device
'                   Attributes; `CSI ? … $ y` — DECRPM, the answer to a mode
'                   query; `CSI <digits;> R` — the cursor position report;
'                   `CSI ? <flags> u` — the kitty keyboard flag report;
'                   `DCS … ST` — XTVERSION and friends; `APC … ST` — the kitty
'                   GRAPHICS answer; `OSC … ST`-or-BEL — colour and palette.
'   reportMouse ... `CSI M` plus exactly three more bytes (X10), `CSI < … M|m`
'                   (SGR), and `CSI <digits;> M` (urxvt).
' The parameter guards are load-bearing in one direction only: they exist so a
' real key is never classified as an answer, never the reverse.
'
' ── A SEQUENCE IS FOUND BY ITS TERMINATOR, NEVER BY A LENGTH ───────────────
' `escEnd` recognises the terminator each introducer actually uses: CSI runs its
' parameters up to a final byte in 0x40..0x7e; DCS, OSC, APC and PM (`ESC P`,
' `ESC ]`, `ESC _`, `ESC ^`) run to ST (`ESC \`) or BEL (0x07). ANYTHING ELSE IS
' TWO BYTES — `ESC` plus one — an Alt-keypress or an introducer proveo does not
' parse. Scanning on past an unknown introducer instead is how one stray ESC
' would swallow the rest of the buffer, keystrokes included.
'
' Incomplete is a third answer, not a rounding of the other two: the fragment is
' neither forwarded nor dropped, it is HELD for the next read. A hold is not
' unconditional: if no continuation arrives within `DefaultEscIdle` the held
' bytes are released VERBATIM, because a fragment nobody continued was never a
' fragment — it was the keypress it looks like.
'
' ── THE WINDOW IS A STATEMENT ABOUT WHO ASKED, NOT ABOUT WHEN ──────────────
' `DefaultReplyWindow` (2s) is how close together two IDENTICAL reports must
' arrive to read as one terminal answering twice rather than as the application
' asking twice. Further apart is a genuine second query, and a genuine second
' query is owed a genuine second answer — so the window is a claim about
' authorship, and the 2s is only how long that claim stays plausible.
'
' ── AND THE OTHER BOUND IS A STATEMENT ABOUT A HUMAN'S FINGERS ─────────────
' `DefaultEscIdle` (40ms) is how long a held fragment waits for its continuation
' before it is forwarded verbatim as the keypress it is. The two constants read
' alike and claim opposite things: `DefaultReplyWindow` is a claim about
' AUTHORSHIP — one terminal answering twice rather than an application asking
' twice — while `DefaultEscIdle` is a claim about FINGERS, that no human types
' the second byte of an Alt-chord 40ms after the first. 25-50ms is the
' conventional terminal ESC timeout (vim's `ttimeoutlen`, kitty's `esc-timeout`)
' and 40ms sits mid-band: long enough that a real Alt-chord, or a CSI split
' across two reads, still assembles; short enough to feel instant.
'
' Waiting at all costs a goroutine. `pumpIn` used to BLOCK in `Read`, so `held`
' was revisited only when more bytes arrived. Press Esc alone and it sat there
' indefinitely; press one more key and the buffer became ESC+key, which `escEnd`
' calls complete at two bytes and forwarded as an ALT-CHORD. Reported by the
' operator as: `proveo run opencode` on macOS, ctrl+p opens the config modal,
' Esc will not close it, and the next keystroke does not interact either.
' The read now runs on its own goroutine feeding a channel (`readChunks` →
' `inRead`, `pumpIn` → `pumpInFrom`), so the pump can `select` against a timer
' armed only while `held` is non-empty, and the released bytes leave through the
' same shared `deliver()` path as everything else. `split()` and
' `classifyTerminalReport` are behaviourally unchanged by this.
'
' ── CPR IS THE SECOND SOLICITED CLASS, AND THE ARGUMENT IS THE SAME ───────
' Added 2026-09-09, and it is the mouse lesson applied a second time rather than
' a new idea. The rule below said of `dropReplies`: "On a prompt stream nothing
' was ever asked, so the first copy is already one too many." That premise is
' TRUE of the reports proveo's own overlay provokes and FALSE of a child that
' asked for itself.
'
' MEASURED, from a cecli run's own startup:
'     WARNING: your terminal doesn't support cursor position requests (CPR).
'     Open documentation url for more info? (Y)es/(N)o/(D)on't ask again [Yes]:
' prompt_toolkit sends `CSI 6n` and blocks on the answer. `keep()` classified the
' `CSI 24;80R` reply as reportReply and `dropReplies` ate it, so the terminal
' looked broken to the agent — and the agent then blocked STARTUP on a Y/N/D
' prompt, which from outside is indistinguishable from a hang.
'
' So CPR gets what mouse got: a discriminator that is OBSERVED STATE, not a flag.
' `cprTracker.observe` watches the CHILD'S OUTPUT for `CSI 6 n` (DSR-CPR) and
' `CSI ? 6 n` (DECXCPR) — 6 only; `CSI 5 n` asks device status and is answered
' with something else. Each query is one credit, spent by the next `CSI …R`,
' and credits are capped at `maxOutstandingCPR` so a child that queries and never
' reads cannot build a standing licence to forward stray reports.
'
' THE CREDIT IS PER CLASS. A cursor query licenses a cursor report and nothing
' else: Device Attributes, DECRPM and the kitty flag report are still dropped
' under `dropReplies`, because nothing asked for those.
'
' The CSI walk both watches need is now `csiScanner`, lifted verbatim out of
' `mouseTracker.observe`. Each watcher holds its OWN scanner, so neither can
' consume the other's carry across a split read.
'
' ── THE MOUSE DISCRIMINATOR IS OBSERVED STATE, NOT A FLAG ──────────────────
' `Proxy.DropReports` is set on EVERY sbx agent run, and `ptyproxy.Usable`
' requires stdin AND stdout to be terminals — so the only production setter of
' that flag fires exclusively on INTERACTIVE runs. It therefore cannot tell a
' "prompt stream" from a "TUI", which is exactly what `keep()` was asking it to
' do while it read `case reportMouse: return !f.dropReplies`. One flag carried
' two unrelated classes, and every press, release, motion and wheel tick was
' discarded before the agent saw it. The tell was in the shape of the code:
' focus got a flag of its own (`dropFocus`), mouse never did — it was lumped in
' with terminal query answers. What actually separates the two cases is not a
' flag anybody sets but STATE anybody can observe: whether the application has
' mouse tracking on right now. The application announces that itself, on its
' own output stream, which is why the filter is no longer input-only.
'
' `mouseTracker.observe` scans the CHILD'S OUTPUT for `CSI ? Ps[;Ps…] h|l`,
' walking parameters and intermediates to the final byte, so a multi-param set
' (`CSI ?1002;1006h`) and one buried inside a repaint are both found. A sequence
' truncated at a read boundary is carried into the next read, bounded by
' `maxModeCarry` (128B, mirroring `maxHeld`) so a long run that never reaches a
' final byte is released rather than hoarded.
'
' Tracking-on is a `uint16` BITMASK of the modes that make a terminal SEND
' reports: 9 (X10 press-only), 1000, 1001, 1002, 1003. A partial disable —
' `?1000;1002h` then `?1002l` — correctly leaves tracking ON, which is the case
' a single boolean gets wrong.
'
' THE ENCODINGS 1005/1006/1015/1016 ARE PARSED AND DELIBERATELY DO NOT LATCH.
' They change a report's SHAPE, never whether one is sent — a terminal emits
' nothing at all for `?1006h` alone. And many TUIs reset the REPORTING modes on
' exit while leaving the ENCODING set, so treating an encoding as "on" would
' latch mouse forwarding on forever after the TUI exits and reintroduce exactly
' the stray-motion corruption the captured trace below pins. This is the
' subtlest decision in the change, and the one a later edit is likeliest to undo.
'
' Concurrency: `on atomic.Bool` plus `mu sync.Mutex`. The atomic carries the one
' datum that crosses goroutines, so `keep()` on the INPUT pump never contends
' with `pumpOut`, which calls `observe` on every single child write; the mutex
' guards the multi-field parser state (`modes` + `carry`) that cannot be atomic
' as a unit. `f.mu` was deliberately NOT reused — it is the reply-dedup lock and
' `keep()`'s reply branch holds it under `defer`. Wiring: `pumpOut()` →
' `pumpOutFrom(io.Reader)` plus `onChildOutput(b)`, which fans each chunk to
' `OutTap` and the tracker; that split is what makes it testable without a pty.
'
' ── WHY IT LOOKED AGENT-SPECIFIC ───────────────────────────────────────────
' The operator saw this on opencode and cecli but NOT on cursor, and asked why,
' given `DropReports` is set backend-wide. Because mouse tracking is a mode the
' APPLICATION turns on, so the three agents were never in the same code path:
'   opencode's captured start-up bundle, now pinned in the tests, is
'   `CSI ?1049h CSI ?1002h CSI ?1006h` — alt screen, button-drag tracking, SGR.
' The moment `?1002h` goes out, the terminal STOPS doing native drag-selection
' and native scrollback-wheel and forwards everything to the app. proveo then
' discarded those reports, so NEITHER side handled the mouse — no selection and
' no scroll. Losing the SCROLL is the tell that this is mouse tracking and not a
' selection quirk; a selection quirk would leave the wheel alone.
' cursor does not emit `?1002h`, so the terminal never hands over, keeps native
' selection and scrollback, and proveo's filter has nothing to drop. cursor was
' unaffected not because proveo treated it differently but because it never
' entered this path at all.
'
' One inherent consequence, which is NOT a proveo bug: while a TUI holds mouse
' tracking, native terminal drag-select stays off. Shift — Option on macOS
' terminals — forces it.
'
' ── THE VERBATIM EVIDENCE THE NOTES BELOW COMPRESS ─────────────────────────
' opencode 1.18.29 opens with BOTH kitty probes. The graphics one goes out as
'   ESC _ Gi=31337,s=1,v=1,a=q,t=d,f=24;AAAA ESC \
' and the keyboard one as `CSI > 5 u`; the terminal's answers to each were
' `reportNone` and arrived as typing.
'
' The read that ended a real crashed session, byte for byte:
'   ^[[200~sk-…^[[201~cERROR: sandbox "…" was stopped
' A bracketed paste, then a lone `c` — the tail of a DA1 reply whose head came
' in the previous read.
'
title Terminal reports — filtered before they become prompts

node "Operator's terminal stack" as TERM <<host>> {
  component "terminal emulator" as EMU <<host>>
  component "multiplexer\nZellij · tmux · screen" as MUX <<error>>
}

node "proveo — ptyproxy" as PX <<host>> {
  component "**classify** — ""classifyTerminalReport""\nlone, self-delimiting reports only" as CLASS <<app>>
  component "**filter** — ""inputFilter.keep""\nwindowed de-duplication" as FILT <<app>>
  component "**tap** — ""PROVEO_TRACE_STDIN""\nrecords arrived + verdict" as TAP <<async>>
  component "**watch** — ""mouseTracker.observe""\n""CSI ? Ps h|l"" off the CHILD'S OUTPUT" as MOUSE <<async>>
}

node "sbx backend — ""DropReports = true""" as SBX <<cloud>> {
  component "agent-session API\ninput read as a PROMPT STREAM" as SESS <<cloud>>
}
node "docker backend · review gate" as DOCK <<app>> {
  component "agent on a REAL tty\nreads reports, discards what it did not ask for" as TTY <<app>>
}
component "Claude Code" as AGENT <<app>>

EMU ARROW_MAIN MUX : keystrokes · query replies
MUX ARROW_ERROR CLASS : one query, TWO answers — or one nobody asked for
CLASS ARROW_MAIN FILT
FILT ARROW_MAIN SBX : keystrokes — plus mouse WHILE the app holds tracking
FILT ARROW_MAIN DOCK : keystrokes + the first of each reply
FILT ARROW_QUEUE TAP : every read, kept or dropped
SBX ARROW_MAIN SESS
SESS ARROW_MAIN AGENT
TTY ARROW_MAIN AGENT
SESS ARROW_ERROR AGENT : an unfiltered report arrives as ""promptSource: sdk""
AGENT ARROW_MAIN MOUSE : child OUTPUT — ""pumpOut → pumpOutFrom → onChildOutput""
MOUSE ARROW_QUEUE FILT : tracking on|off, an ""atomic.Bool"" — the OUTPUT→INPUT edge
MOUSE ARROW_MAIN EMU : the same bytes, painted unchanged

note bottom of MUX
  **Measured, not assumed.** In run ""proveo-1787703005-59852"" the tap recorded
  Zellij answering ONE Primary Device Attributes query twice — two identical
  ""\\x1b[?62;4c"" reads 42ms apart — plus focus reports nothing downstream consumes.

  The surplus has no query to belong to. Whatever reads the stream must treat it
  as input, because input is the only thing it can be.

  **A duplicate was never the requirement.** Run ""proveo-1787852436-14907"" died
  the same way with no duplicate anywhere: the tap recorded ONE ""\\x1b[?6c"" —
  a VT102 Device Attributes reply — five seconds into a run, ""sent"", while the
  focus reports 3s and 8s later were both ""DROPPED"". A lone report is
  indistinguishable from a legitimate answer under de-duplication alone, so it
  was forwarded as "the first copy" and enqueued. The agent answered a ""/color""
  nobody typed and exited; the tail's last bytes were the report's trailing ""c"".

  This needs no multiplexer and no second instance. One unsolicited report is
  the whole mechanism.

  **And reports are not the only shape of it.** The same tap on run
  ""proveo-1787868039-64880"" recorded 65 SGR mouse-motion reads —
  ""\x1b[<35;1;46M"" through ""\x1b[<35;50;39M"", coordinates drifting as a hand
  moved across the window — every one ""sent"", because ""classifyTerminalReport""
  knew nothing ending in ""M"". On a prompt stream they land in the input line as
  noise. Mouse tracking was enabled BY the agent, so this is a report the far end
  asked for and cannot receive: solicited at one end of the hop and meaningless
  at the other.
end note

note right of SESS
  **Why an ignorable stray becomes a user message.** A terminal application
  discards a report it did not ask for. sbx does not run the agent as a terminal
  application: it drives it over the agent-session API, where input is a PROMPT
  STREAM. The transcript records the result as
  ""promptSource: sdk · entrypoint: sdk-cli"" — enqueued, dequeued, answered.

  It never reaches ""history\.jsonl"", because that records what the TUI submitted
  and the TUI submitted nothing. That absence is how the injection was found.
end note

note left of FILT
  **Three rules, and the first one belongs to the BACKEND.**

  ""dropReplies"" (""Proxy.DropReports"") — drop every UNSOLICITED report. Set
  on the sbx path and nowhere else. De-duplication cannot cover that backend:
  it decides which COPY is surplus, having granted the first is owed, and
  where nothing was asked the first copy is already one too many.
  QUALIFIED 2026-09-09: a ""CSI …R"" whose ""CSI 6n"" was seen on the child's
  own output IS owed, and outranks this. See the CPR section above.

  ""reportReply"" (default) — forward the FIRST, drop copies inside
  ""DefaultReplyWindow"" (2s). The application asked and is owed one answer;
  only the surplus goes. De-duplication is windowed rather than
  adjacent-only, because the captured duplicate had a mode report between
  it and its twin. This is the DOCKER contract, where the agent holds a real
  tty and a withheld answer leaves it waiting.

  ""reportFocus"" — dropped outright. A focus notification is neither a
  keystroke nor an answer, and nothing past this point consumes one. That it
  was unconditional is why focus never caused this and replies did.

  ""reportMouse"" — DEC 9/1000/1001/1002/1003, in all four encodings. SOLICITED,
  unlike the other two: the application turns tracking ON itself, and while it
  holds it a click, drag or wheel tick is input it asked for. So this is the one
  class that does NOT branch on ""dropReplies"" alone. The discriminator is the
  OBSERVED tracking state — tracking on, forward; tracking off, fall back to
  ""!dropReplies"" as before. Never de-duplicated either way: every motion is a
  distinct event, not a surplus copy of one.
  ""CSI M Cb Cx Cy"" is matched on its LENGTH because its coordinates are raw
  bytes; the other three end in ""M"" or ""m"" and match like the rest.

  A read carrying a report ALONGSIDE anything else is forwarded untouched.
  Eating a keystroke to win a filtering argument is far worse than passing a
  stray report through — and terminals emit reports as one atomic write, which
  is what makes the whole-read rule safe.

  ""PROVEO_STDIN_FILTER=off"" restores byte-for-byte passthrough.
end note

note bottom of TAP
  The tap exists because a child-side log cannot show what was DROPPED, and a
  transcript cannot show what ARRIVED. Wrapping ""os.Stdin"" to find out would
  make ""os/exec"" substitute a pipe and take the agent's tty away — changing the
  very behaviour under investigation. Through the pty the child keeps its
  terminal either way.
end note
note as N_KITTY_GAP
  **Two replies it did not know, and opencode
  provokes BOTH on startup.** A kitty GRAPHICS
  probe (APC) and a kitty KEYBOARD push
  (""CSI > 5 u""), each answered by the terminal.
end note

note as N_KITTY_APC
  ""ESC _ Gi=31337;OK ESC\\"" is APC — ""b[1] == '_'""
  was simply absent from the switch, so the
  answer classified as ""reportNone"" and reached
  the agent as keystrokes.
end note

note as N_KITTY_U
  ""ESC [ ? <flags> u"" had no ""'u'"" case either.
  The guard is ""body[0] == '?'"": a kitty-encoded
  KEYPRESS is ""CSI 1;5 u"" with no '?', and
  swallowing real keys would be the worse bug.
end note

note as N_KITTY_SEEN
  Observed in a live session: the pane filling
  with ""Gi=31337,…"", ""^[[18~"" (F7), ""^[[19~"" (F8)
  and bare digits — terminal answers arriving as
  input.
end note

note as N_KITTY_TAIL
  And every crash ended ""cERROR: sandbox … was
  stopped"" — that stray ""c"" is the tail of a DA1
  reply whose prefix went elsewhere. Three
  sightings before it was read as a clue.
end note

note as N_MOUSE_MODES
  Tracking-on is 9/1000/1001/1002/1003 only.
  1005/1006/1015/1016 set the report SHAPE —
  a terminal sends nothing for them alone, and
  TUIs exit leaving them ON. Latching one would
  forward mouse forever after the TUI quit.
end note

note as N_MOUSE_WHY
  opencode opens ""?1049h ?1002h ?1006h"": the
  terminal hands the mouse over, so native
  select AND scrollback stop. cursor sends no
  ""?1002h"" and never entered this path at all.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_UNIT
  **The filter judges a SEQUENCE, not a read.**
  ""keep()"" took one whole ""Read()"" and answered
  yes or no for all of it, which is wrong in two
  directions at once.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_TAIL
  A reply split across reads left a fragment:
  ""ESC[?62;"" then ""c"". The second piece failed
  the ""len < 3"" guard and reached the agent as a
  keystroke.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_SEEN
  That ""c"" sat in front of every ""sandbox was
  stopped"" in this investigation — seen three
  times and dismissed three times before it was
  read as evidence.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_BOTH
  And a read carrying a report BESIDE real typing
  was all-or-nothing, so filtering it ate the
  keystrokes too.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_PASTE
  Bracketed paste makes both routine: the text,
  its ""ESC[200~""/""ESC[201~"" markers and whatever
  the terminal was still answering all land in
  one read.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_HOLD
  An unfinished sequence is HELD for the next
  read. ""maxHeld"" (128B) bounds SIZE only, so no
  run hoards the buffer — but it never released a
  lone ESC, which is ONE byte. Time bounds that.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_IDLE
  ""DefaultEscIdle"" (40ms) is the other bound. On
  idle the hold is forwarded VERBATIM, so Esc
  closes a modal instead of waiting to be glued
  to the next key as a phantom Alt-chord.
end note

@enduml
